<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fianarantsoa</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fianarantsoa is a city in the southern highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and the main town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsileo">Betsileo</a> region. It was founded in 1830 by the Merina queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a> as an administrative centre to control the conquered Betsileo, and it became a centre of education, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and wine-making. A railway links it to Manakara on the east coast.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fianarantsoa.jpg" alt="Fianarantsoa" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: HoneyGaLe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_globale_de_la_ville_Fianarantsoa_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fianarantsoa lies at an average altitude of 1,200 metres in south central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> and is the capital of the Haute Matsiatra Region. Two rivers, the Mandranofotsy in the west and the Tsiandanitra in the east, cross the town; both flow into the Matsiatra river. National road 7, one of the main highways of the island, links it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, 411 km away, and to Toliara, 518 km away, and a railway runs from the town to Manakara on the coast. The town has 191,766 inhabitants. Its name means good education in Malagasy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Merina built Fianarantsoa in the early 19th century as the administrative capital of the newly conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/betsileo">Betsileo</a> kingdoms. It grew into a cultural and intellectual centre for the island. It has some of the oldest Protestant and Lutheran cathedrals in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, the island's oldest theological seminary, also Lutheran, and the seat of the Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Fianarantsoa. A university, named after the town, was built there in 1972, and the many wine producers of the town have given it a reputation as the wine capital of Madagascar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Upper Town, the historic centre, has brick houses renovated in their original style and several churches along Rue du Rova. The oldest church in the town, Eglise Antranobiriky, dates from 1859. The largest, the Cathedral of the Holy Name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>, was built from 1871 to 1890, and the neogothic Protestant church Fiangonana Trinité Masombahoaka dates from 1885. The railway station in the Lower Town was inaugurated in 1935, and a memorial in front of it honours the victims of the Malagasy Uprising of 1947. Ranomafana National Park lies 65 km to the north-east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fianarantsoa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lalangina was one of four main Betsileo kingdoms that took shape in the southern highlands of Madagascar in the 18th century, in the region around modern <b>Fianarantsoa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lalangina">Kingdom of Lalangina</a></li></ul></section>
